code.code:
type Preference = 'light' | 'dark' | 'system';

// Client island: starts after hydration and after the first paint.
export function bootstrapTheme() {
  const stored = localStorage.getItem('theme') as Preference | null;
  const preference: Preference = stored ?? 'system';
  const mql = matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');

  document.documentElement.dataset.theme =
    preference === 'system' ? (mql.matches ? 'dark' : 'light') : preference;
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Four symptoms, three of them from one root cause: the theme is decided after paint, from a value the server never saw, and then never revisited.

**1. The flash is a timing defect.** The browser paints as soon as it has CSS and body content; an island that boots after hydration is later than that, so the first frames use whatever the CSS defaults resolve to. The decision has to happen before the body paints: a small blocking script in `<head>`, or the server rendering `data-theme` from a cookie. Anything that waits on a module graph, an effect or `DOMContentLoaded` loses the race by construction.

**2. The mismatch is the same boundary seen from the server.** The server sends `<html>` without `data-theme` (or with its own default) and the client sets a different value, so two different trees are compared. Pick one owner for the attribute: either the server renders it from the cookie the client also reads, or the framework never renders it and only the blocking script sets it. Never both with different inputs.

**3. No subscription.** `mql` is read once. `system` is a live preference, so a `change` listener must re-resolve — but only while the preference is still `system`, and it must be removed with the island.

**4. The cast is a lie.** `as Preference` validates nothing; `auto` from an older build flows through, sets `data-theme="auto"`, and no selector matches, so the page falls back to the base tokens. Validate, then default to `system`.

The boundary to state in review: `Preference` is what the user chose and what is persisted (`light | dark | system`); `Resolved` is derived (`light | dark`) and is never persisted. Put the pure part in a module both halves share and keep DOM work at the edges.

```ts
// theme.ts - shared, no DOM, no storage
export type Preference = 'light' | 'dark' | 'system';
export type Resolved = 'light' | 'dark';

export const PREFERENCE_KEY = 'theme';

export const isPreference = (v: unknown): v is Preference =>
  v === 'light' || v === 'dark' || v === 'system';

export function resolve(preference: Preference, systemPrefersDark: boolean): Resolved {
  return preference === 'system' ? (systemPrefersDark ? 'dark' : 'light') : preference;
}
```

```html
<!-- <head>, before any body markup: synchronous and tiny -->
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      var p = localStorage.getItem('theme');
      if (p !== 'light' && p !== 'dark' && p !== 'system') p = 'system';
      var dark = matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
      var root = document.documentElement;
      root.dataset.themePreference = p;
      root.dataset.theme = p === 'system' ? (dark ? 'dark' : 'light') : p;
    } catch (e) {
      /* storage blocked: keep the CSS default, which must be a complete theme */
    }
  })();
</script>
```

```ts
// theme-client.ts - island, after hydration
import { PREFERENCE_KEY, isPreference, resolve, type Preference } from './theme';

const mql = matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');
const root = document.documentElement;

const readPreference = (): Preference => {
  const raw = localStorage.getItem(PREFERENCE_KEY);
  return isPreference(raw) ? raw : 'system';
};

const paint = (resolved: 'light' | 'dark') => { root.dataset.theme = resolved; };

function onSystemChange() {
  if (readPreference() === 'system') paint(resolve('system', mql.matches));
}

mql.addEventListener('change', onSystemChange);
// island teardown: mql.removeEventListener('change', onSystemChange);

export function setPreference(next: Preference) {
  localStorage.setItem(PREFERENCE_KEY, next); // the preference, never the resolved value
  root.dataset.themePreference = next;
  paint(resolve(next, mql.matches));
}
```

```css
:root { color-scheme: light; --surface: #fff; --text: #111; }
[data-theme="dark"] { color-scheme: dark; --surface: #111; --text: #eee; }
```

Two details that keep the fix honest. The CSS default must be a complete, readable theme, because it is what a blocked `localStorage` degrades to. And when the server owns the attribute, it reads the same validated preference from a cookie (`SameSite=Lax`, readable before render) and applies the same default; the inline script then only confirms it, so server HTML and first paint already agree and nothing is patched after hydration.

code.code:
/* vendor.css - loaded by a <link> in the app shell, unlayered */
.widget .title { color: red; }

/* app.css - bundled */
@layer components {
  :where(.Card) .title {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
}

/* The vendor <link> and app.css are both normal author styles, and nothing
   in the current setup says which one belongs to whom. */

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Precedence for normal declarations is origin and importance, then layer, then specificity, then order of appearance. Unlayered author CSS sits after every layer, so `.widget .title` is not competing on specificity at all: it wins because the whole `components` layer is below the unlayered space. That is also why `.Card .title.title` does nothing — you are raising a number the cascade never consults.

The policy: one ordered list, declared once in the entry stylesheet, with every stylesheet routed into it.

```css
/* app.css - first lines of the entry sheet */
@layer reset, vendor, components, app;

@import url("vendor.css") layer(vendor); /* before every rule */

@layer components {
  :where(.Card) .title {
    color: var(--color-text, canvastext);
  }
}

@layer app {
  .checkout .Card .title {
    /* later layer: this is where product-specific overrides belong */
  }
}
```

Two ordering rules carry the fix. `@import` must precede all rules except `@charset` and `@layer` statements, or it is ignored — hence the layer statement first and the import immediately after it (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@import). And layer order is fixed the first time those names appear, so declaring it in the entry file makes the layers independent of bundler import order; verify in the built CSS anyway, because a bundler can hoist or inline the import.

For a vendor sheet that arrives through a `<link>` there is no way to wrap it from HTML. Either move it into the entry as `@import ... layer(vendor)` (the pipeline this repo already runs supports it) or wrap its content in `@layer vendor { ... }` during the build. Pick one path and make the vendored file a build-time dependency instead of an ambient link that may or may not be present on a given page.

`:where()` stays. It contributes zero specificity, which is the point once layers own precedence: the component rule should be easy for the app layer to override. And note the inversion: `!important` reverses layer order, so an `!important` inside `vendor` would outrank an `!important` inside `app`. Importing the vendor into a layer does not neutralize vendor `!important` — audit those and fix them at the package boundary instead of escalating.

Verify with a route that has a real conflict — vendor rule, component rule, app override — asserting computed `color` on the built bundle, not on the dev server, because that is where the concatenation order actually exists.

code.code:
type ButtonProps = {
  tone?: 'brand' | 'danger';
  size?: 'sm' | 'md';
  className?: string;
  children: React.ReactNode;
};

export function Button({ tone = 'brand', size = 'md', className, children }: ButtonProps) {
  // Runtime engine: generates a name such as `css-1x2y3` and attaches it to the root node.
  return renderWithRuntimeCssInJs({ tone, size, className, children });
}

// A product call site found by the audit:
const ToolbarButton = styled(Button)`
  & .css-1x2y3 { margin-inline-end: var(--space-2); }
`;

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The boundary is the exported component, not the engine. `Button` keeps its name and its props; what changes is what it renders and what it promises. Everything the audit found is a leaked implementation: a hash inside a consumer selector, style tags counted as output, a theme object read as state. None of that is contract, so the migration is about replacing the leaks with documented hooks rather than reproducing them.

**Order of work.** Tokens first, because they survive any engine and give the new CSS something to consume. Then leaf primitives. Then the two couplings. The runtime goes last, when nothing imports it.

**The public hook is an attribute plus tokens, not a class name.**

```tsx
// button/Button.tsx - static implementation, same public props
import { cx } from '../../utils/cx';

export function Button({ tone = 'brand', size = 'md', className, children }: ButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button type="button" data-tone={tone} data-size={size} className={cx('btn', className)}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
}
```

```css
/* button/button.css - every value is a token; the attribute is the hook */
.btn {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: var(--space-2);
  padding-inline: var(--space-3);
  border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  background: var(--btn-bg, var(--color-action-bg));
  color: var(--color-action-fg);
}
.btn[data-tone="danger"] { --btn-bg: var(--color-danger-bg); }
.btn[data-size="sm"] { padding-inline: var(--space-2); }
```

**The 41 descendant selectors.** Do not reproduce `css-1x2y3`; it is a build artifact and will change again. Each site gets a documented hook with the same meaning — usually `data-size`/`data-tone` on the root, or a local wrapper class when the spacing is layout the component should not own:

```css
/* product code after the codemod: same intent, documented hook */
.toolbar .btn { margin-inline-end: var(--space-2); }
```

**The theme-object readers.** They wanted a value, not engine state. If they need a color at runtime, read the token from CSS (`getComputedStyle(root).getPropertyValue('--color-...')`); if they need to pick an icon variant, read the resolved theme attribute. Export a small token map only for values JS genuinely cannot get from CSS.

**SSR.** Static extraction gives one deterministic stylesheet in a `<link>`: no per-render injection, so the 40 kB of duplicates disappears and server and client HTML agree. Confirm the built CSS keeps the layer order and that no component CSS arrives through a runtime import.

**Deletion criteria.** Zero imports of `styled`/`css`/`ThemeProvider` (lint rule from day one), the SSR injection path removed, visual baselines passing with the static output, and the engine gone from the bundle. An adapter for sites that cannot move yet is fine, but give it an owner and a removal date — an adapter with no date becomes the architecture.

code.code:
/* tokens.css */
:root {
  --text-card-title: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 2vw, 2.5rem);
  --space-card: clamp(1rem, 0.75rem + 2cqi, 2rem);
}

/* card.css */
.card {
  container-type: inline-size;
  padding: var(--space-card);
  display: grid;
  gap: var(--space-card);
}

.card__title {
  font-size: var(--text-card-title);
}

@container (min-width: 34rem) {
  .card__title {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.4rem + 1vw, 2.75rem);
  }
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Three separate defects, and only the third is a layout bug — the first two are the same misunderstanding from opposite directions: which box the value is measured against.

**1. The title follows the viewport, not the card.** `--text-card-title` puts `2vw` in the preferred term, so the computed size depends on the viewport only. Sidebar and grid column resolve to the same length because placement does not participate. The container query at `34rem` does switch the value, but both branches are still viewport-driven, and a single `vw` step cannot track a card that is resized by a grid track. The fix is `cqi` in the preferred term — 1% of the nearest query container's inline size — with the bounds kept in `rem` so user font-size and zoom still scale the result.

**2. An element is not its own query container.** `--space-card` is consumed by `.card` while `.card` is the element that declares `container-type: inline-size`. Container query length units resolve against the nearest *ancestor* query container; an element's own containment does not feed its own properties. With no ancestor container in these placements there is no eligible container, and per spec the container length unit falls back to the small viewport unit for that axis — so the padding silently behaves like `sv*`, i.e. viewport-derived, which is exactly the reported "padding ignores the card". Either the consumer is a descendant of the container (an inner wrapper) or the containment lives on an ancestor slot.

**3. Inline-size containment removes content from inline sizing.** `container-type: inline-size` applies layout, style and inline-size containment. In a block context with a definite width that is invisible; in a shrink-to-fit context — `inline-block`, a float, a `max-content` track — the inline size can no longer be derived from the contents, so the card resolves to zero (plus padding). Containment belongs on an element that already has a definite inline size: the layout slot, not the component that must sometimes size to its content.

Refactor: one wrap-once layer order, tokens that carry the fluid decision, containment on the slot, and a static fallback for engines without container queries.

```css
@layer tokens, system, app;

@layer tokens {
  :root {
    /* bounds in rem: zoom and user font size still scale the result */
    --text-card-title: clamp(1.375rem, 1rem + 2.5cqi, 2.5rem);
    --space-card: clamp(1rem, 0.75rem + 1.5cqi, 2rem);
  }
}

@layer system {
  /* the slot owns containment: it has a definite inline size */
  .card-slot { container: card / inline-size; }

  .card {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.25rem;              /* no-container fallback */
  }

  .card__title { font-size: 1.5rem; }

  @supports (container-type: inline-size) {
    /* cqi in a :root token is fine: substitution happens at the use site,
       so each card resolves against its own .card-slot */
    .card { padding: var(--space-card); }
    .card__title { font-size: var(--text-card-title); }
  }

  @container card (inline-size >= 34rem) {
    .card { grid-template-columns: 12rem 1fr; }
  }
}
```

Why this shape and not another:

- **Tokens stay the contract.** Components read `--text-card-title` / `--space-card`; the fluid formula is not re-typed inside the container query, so a designer changing the ramp changes every card. A component-local `clamp()` with raw numbers is drift, not a design decision.
- **`cqi` at `:root` is not a mistake.** Custom property values are substituted before resolution, so the unit resolves on the consumer's box, against `.card-slot`. That is what makes one token correct for a sidebar card and a grid card at the same time.
- **Containment sits on the slot, with a name.** `container: card / inline-size` keeps the query scoped; an unnamed `@container (min-width: 34rem)` binds to whichever ancestor container happens to be nearest, which is how a modal's card starts inheriting the modal's width. The chip-row placement stays outside the containment tree or gets an explicit width — a shrink-to-fit box cannot both contain and size to content.
- **Two fallbacks, both explicit.** The plain `padding`/`font-size` before the `@supports` block covers engines without container units; the `@container` block is ignored wholesale by engines without container queries, leaving the static typography instead of a broken layout.
- **Verification.** Stories per container width (20rem / 34rem / 60rem), 200% zoom with long unbroken strings, a 320px viewport, and a forced `@supports`-off run to see the fallback. Fluid type fails at the extremes, not in the middle.

Sources: MDN container queries — `container-type`, container query length units and the small-viewport fallback (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_containment/Container_queries), MDN `clamp()` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/clamp), MDN `container-type` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/container-type), MDN `@container` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/@container).
